course: dictionary
lesson: 11133
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around the word first. «In cricket the bowler tries to hit the stumps and the batter defends them, scoring runs by running between wickets 22 yards apart.» Here the sentence is about cricket and running, so wicket means one of the two sets of stumps.
explain: Cricket has its own words. A wicket is the set of three upright sticks, called stumps, with two small pieces, called bails, on top. The bowler tries to knock the bails off. When a batter is out, the team has taken a wicket.
explain: Look at the numbers too. «150 for 6» means 150 runs and 6 batters out. So a wicket can also be a count of batters out, and the number tells you how many.
explain: Wicket can mean something outside sport too. A wicket gate is a small door or gate, often in or beside a bigger one. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A wicket is a set of three stumps with two bails on top.
fact: Wickets are 22 yards apart, and batters run between them to score runs.
fact: «Six wickets» means six batters are out.
fact: A sticky wicket is a difficult situation, not a muddy pitch.
fact: A wicket gate is a small gate, often beside a bigger one.
exercise: In cricket, what is a wicket? | A bat made of willow | *The set of three stumps with two bails on top | A player who bowls the ball | A helmet with a face guard
exercise: In the score «150 for 6», what does the 6 mean? | 6 players on the team | *6 batters out | 6 runs scored | 6 overs bowled
exercise: What does «take a wicket» mean? | To bowl a wide | *To get a batter out | To run between the stumps | To hit the ball for six
test: In the phrase «a sticky wicket», what does wicket mean? | A loose stump | A wet, muddy pitch | A cricket match played indoors | *A difficult situation
test: What is a wicket gate? | A large gate in a field | *A small door or gate, often beside a bigger one | A set of stumps at the end of a pitch | A bat with a long handle
exam: Why must young batters wear a helmet with a face guard? | To keep the bails on the stumps | *To protect the face when batting against a hard ball | To look smart on the pitch | To make the bowler's ball go faster
